    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Woodcut is Big Big Train's 16th studio album and their first full-fledged concept album, spanning 66 minutes across 16 tracks. The album tells the story of The Artist, a tortured woodcarver and printmaker who becomes consumed by his own creation, blurring the line between reality and imagination as he grapples with the anxieties of artistic creation. Rather than relying on extended instrumental passages, the band employed a more functional songwriting approach, with each track serving the narrative while maintaining distinct musical identity and containing unexpected twists that sustain listener engagement.\n\nMusically, Woodcut showcases Big Big Train's signature blend of melodic and symphonic rock with folk influences, featuring intricate orchestrations that weave together electric guitars, keyboards, strings, brass, and vocals in organic symbiosis. The album employs recurring melodic themes and motifs throughout—introduced as early as the opening instrumental \"Inkwell Black\"—that musically and lyrically connect the listener to the protagonist's journey. Standout tracks like \"The Artist,\" \"Warp and Weft\" (featuring 7/8 time signatures and experimental vocal arrangements), and the chaotic instrumental \"Cut and Run\" showcase the band's ability to balance complexity with emotional resonance, avoiding gratuitous showmanship in favor of arrangements that intentionally serve the story.\n\nThe album represents a significant artistic statement for the band, combining classical prog rock sensibilities with contemporary songwriting discipline. Featuring vocalist Alberto Bravin in his second appearance with the group, Woodcut has been widely praised as a triumph of modern progressive rock that feels both epic and purposeful, making it an ideal entry point for newcomers while satisfying longtime fans with its sophisticated musicianship and narrative depth.",
